<h2>How to Prevent Heart Attacks When You're Young</h2>
<p>The encouraging news is that the majority of early heart attacks are preventable. Here is what genuinely works.</p>

<h3>1. Quit Smoking and All Tobacco</h3>
<p>There is no safe level of smoking or vaping for your heart. Quitting is the fastest, most powerful thing you can do — your risk starts falling within days and continues dropping for years.</p>

<h3>2. Know Your Numbers</h3>
<p>Even in your 20s and 30s, get your blood pressure, blood sugar (fasting glucose or HbA1c), and cholesterol (lipid profile) checked. These silent conditions are easy to treat once you know about them. If you have a family history of early heart disease, start screening sooner.</p>

<h3>3. Eat a Heart-Smart Diet</h3>
<p>Reduce ultra-processed foods, deep-fried snacks, refined carbohydrates, sugary drinks, and excess salt. Fill your plate with vegetables, fruit, whole grains, pulses, nuts, and healthy fats. Small, consistent changes matter more than crash diets.</p>

<h3>4. Move Every Day</h3>
<p>Aim for at least 150 minutes of moderate exercise per week — brisk walking, cycling, swimming, or sport. Regular activity lowers blood pressure, improves cholesterol, controls weight, and reduces stress. If you have symptoms or existing risk factors, get a cardiac check before starting an intense new programme.</p>

<h3>5. Manage Stress and Sleep</h3>
<p>Protect 7–8 hours of quality sleep, and build real stress-management habits — exercise, breathing techniques, meditation, or talking to someone. Chronic stress is not "just in your head"; it has measurable effects on your heart.</p>

<h3>6. Limit Alcohol and Avoid Recreational Drugs</h3>
<p>Keep alcohol within safe limits and completely avoid stimulant drugs, which can trigger a heart attack even in a healthy young person.</p>

<h3>7. Don't Ignore Symptoms</h3>
<p>If something feels wrong — persistent chest discomfort, breathlessness on mild exertion, palpitations, or unexplained fatigue — get it checked. It is always better to be evaluated and reassured than to dismiss a warning sign.</p>

<blockquote>"You are never 'too young' for a heart attack — but you are also never too young to prevent one. The habits you build in your 20s and 30s decide the health of your heart for the next fifty years."</blockquote>

<h2>When to See a Doctor</h2>
<p>Book a cardiac evaluation if you have a family history of early heart disease, if you smoke or have diabetes, high blood pressure, or high cholesterol, or if you experience chest discomfort, breathlessness, palpitations, or fainting. A simple check-up — ECG, blood pressure, blood tests, and sometimes an echocardiogram or stress test — can reveal hidden risk long before a crisis. For a broader guide, see our article on <a href="/blog/heart-health"><strong>keeping your heart healthy</strong></a>.</p>

